<h2>The suit and the proposed amendments</h2>
<p>The plaintiff sued for specific performance of three Agreements to Sell of April, May and December 2017 relating to a property in Greater Kailash-I, pleading that he was a tenant of the premises under earlier leases and that part of the consideration was to be discharged by transferring agricultural land in Punjab. In 2021 he sought to amend the plaint to plead, among other things, cash payments of Rs 60 lakh, an adjustment of Rs 1.5 crore as the value of the Punjab land, and revised figures of the total consideration. The Single Judge refused the amendments in January 2024, and the plaintiff appealed to the Division Bench.</p>

<h2>The Division Bench&rsquo;s analysis</h2>
<p>The Bench of Justices Vivek Chaudhary and Renu Bhatnagar restated the settled framework: amendments are allowed liberally before trial, refused where they change the nature of the suit or set up a fundamentally different case, with <em>LIC v. Sanjeev Builders</em>, (2022) 16 SCC 1, as the governing authority. Applying it, the Bench looked past the individual paragraphs to the combined effect of the amendments:</p>
<div class="tiles">
  <div class="tile"><strong>Explanatory on their face, transformative in combination.</strong> Some amendments, viewed alone, merely elaborated; read with the new payment case, they re-founded the suit on a different account of consideration.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>New cash payments.</strong> The proposed plea of Rs 60 lakh in cash was a new, specific case of payment — not a quantification of something already pleaded.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>A valuation appearing for the first time.</strong> The Rs 1.5 crore value assigned to the Punjab land was new, and the revised total consideration left an unexplained gap of Rs 67 lakh against the figure in the first agreement.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>A changed explanation.</strong> Even the reason pleaded for the reduction in consideration had shifted from the original plaint, and one amended paragraph stated the figure inconsistently in words and numerals.</div>
</div>
<p>Taken together, the amendments introduced a materially different factual account of the consideration and its discharge. The appeal was dismissed, with no order as to costs.</p>

<h2>Why this matters in specific performance litigation</h2>
<p>In a specific performance suit, the plaintiff&rsquo;s pleading of the agreed consideration and of his own payments is not peripheral detail — it is the core of readiness and willingness under Section 16(c) of the Specific Relief Act, 1963. Courts are therefore especially alert to amendments that rework this core after the defendant&rsquo;s written statement has exposed its weaknesses. The practical lessons are familiar but bear repeating: plead the complete payment history, with modes and dates, at the institution of the suit; where genuine elaboration later becomes necessary, tie every new figure expressly to something already pleaded; and treat internal inconsistencies between figures and words as the litigation hazards they are, because appellate benches notice them.</p>
<div class="note">Order VI Rule 17 CPC and its proviso — which bars post-trial amendments absent due diligence — apply to civil suits generally; in commercial suits the Commercial Courts Act, 2015 framework makes courts stricter still. An appeal to the Division Bench under Section 10 of the Delhi High Court Act lies from original-side orders of a Single Judge of the kind involved here. This article is informational and not advice in any matter.</div>
